1. Make a better mask

Pick a frame with no Scherrer rings (the first LaB6 frame or a closed-beam one, as you suggested). Check it looks dark, with only isolated bright dots.
Open it in pyFAI-drawmask (or the mask tool in pyFAI-calib2), mask the module gaps and every hot or dead pixel and short line you can see, then save it as .edf or .npy. Non-zero means masked.
Include the detector’s own mask, or OR them together. Draw a small margin around the beam stop and any shadowed edges.
In Cell 1, point the mask setting (BASE_EDF_MASK or EXTRA_MASK_FILE) at the new file. Set REBUILD_MASK = True for one run, then back to False.
Clear outputs/cache/ (or set FORCE_RECOMPUTE) so everything is re-integrated with the new mask.

2. Redo the geometry

In pyFAI-calib2, calibrate on the LaB6 with the same energy and detector. Use many rings, refine the distance, PONI, rot1/rot2/rot3, and check that every ring is well fitted.
Save the new .poni and select it in the notebook. A tilt or PONI error smears the peaks across χ, which adds to the apparent instrument width.

3. Improve the LaB6 fits (Cell 4)

Check the residual and η for each peak in the printed table. The 111, 200 and 210 fits were the poor ones.
Print the FWHM in pixels and in 2θ for each peak, and plot width against q. It should be a smooth trend.
Fit LaB6 in narrow χ sectors, such as 10° wedges, and compare the widths. If they’re clearly narrower, the integrated width includes χ smearing, and you need to decide whether your sample integration range should match the LaB6 one.
Make sure you integrate the LaB6 over the same χ range as the samples. Otherwise the width doesn’t transfer.
Exclude peaks that fit badly from the extrapolation, using LAB6_MAX_RMS_PCT.

4. Check the extrapolation to q ≈ 0.99

Your lowest LaB6 peak is (100) at 1.51 Å⁻¹, so the width at 0.99 is extrapolated.
Extrapolate the total FWHM and η (smooth in q) instead of H_G and H_L separately, and compare the two results.
Include the uncertainty of the extrapolation in the L_c CIs, not a flat 3 %.

5. Check that it transfers to the samples

Same energy, detector distance, geometry and χ range for the LaB6 and the sample integration.
The beam footprint at grazing incidence can add width that a LaB6 powder doesn’t have, so keep that in mind as a possible leftover.
Compare the instrument width against the narrowest real sample peaks. If any sample peak is narrower than the instrument width, something is off.

6. Rerun and re-check

Rerun Cells 4, 5, 6, 7, 7B, 7C, 7D and 8. Compare L_c, η and the number of resolution-limited samples before and after.
Keep the old results in a separate folder so you can see what changed.

